Worked in an independent `cp -a` of /tmp/budget2-gv2-snap at /tmp/gv2-check (HEAD 5fae968, matches stated base commit; local `master` ref in the snapshot is a stale unrelated pointer at 79c3c3a — diffed against 5fae968 instead, which cleanly matches the manifest, see scope check below).

ORACLE (independent re-run, not the lead's file): `.swarm/tier3/GV2/accept.sh /tmp/gv2-check` → full build/vet/test suite green, all four TestGV2Oracle* PASS, GM-run marker-contract regression PASS, last line `ORACLE PASS`. (Note: the lead's own oracle.1.log was mid-write/being regenerated concurrently while I read it the first time, producing a truncated-looking read; a second read after completion showed it also ends `ORACLE PASS`. My own independent run in my own copy is the evidence of record.)

Attack-by-attack:

1. MC/backtest/nil-cost. Read monte_carlo.go:466-482 and backtest.go:255-260 — both only read `out.FundedLivingExpenses`, `out.TotalBalance`, `out.LivingExpenses`, `out.GuardrailMultiplier`, `out.Result.*`, `out.AllowTaxDeferredWithdrawal`; neither reads GuardrailPeak/Baseline/CutTrigger/RaiseTrigger. stepper.go's `Guardrails.Evaluate` call (line 295) and `activeMultiplier` derivation are byte-identical to before; the four new fields are purely local, read-only projections of already-existing `st.Guardrails` state computed AFTER Evaluate, never fed back into it. Structurally no divergence is possible; confirmed by the full green `go test ./internal/services/...` (73s retirement, 55s analysis) in my own build. `st.Guardrails == nil` path: the four locals stay float64 zero-value, `if st.Guardrails != nil` skips all guardrail work — no added cost.

2. Chain transitions. Read stepper.go: `s := st.active` is reassigned to the post-transition `prepared` settings BEFORE the guardrail-trigger block (lines 236-246, then 318-325 uses `s.Guardrails`), so triggers already used ACTIVE settings. Verified empirically with a throwaway probe test (`engine` package, custom `Hooks.ResolveChainTransition` switching FloorDropPct 10%→40% at year 3, no scenario files needed): year 2 cut trigger matched primary-derived value, year 3 matched LINKED-derived value and did NOT match the primary-derived value (`gv2Close` false) — confirms the active-settings config is actually used, not silently inherited. Probe removed after.

3. Depletion. Two throwaway fixtures in the `whatif` package: (a) depletion after 2 months, 1 yearly summary; (b) depletion within month 0 of year 0 (1 month total, 1 yearly summary). `buildProjectionChartData` called in both nominal and real modes with `recover()` wrapping — no panic, `len(x)==len(y)` on every trace in both cases. The `idx := (i+1)*12-1; if idx > lastMonthIdx { idx = lastMonthIdx }` clamp and the marker `monthIdx` clamp both hold at the boundary. Probes removed after.

4. Real-mode divisor. Trace loop (handlers.go:814-820) and marker-relocation Y (day-820, 875-883) both divide by `projection.Months[idx].CumulativeInflation` using the SAME `idx = min((i+1)*12-1, lastMonthIdx)` formula; the summary line's `last.CumulativeInflation` (handlers.go:617) comes from `finalizeCurrentYear`'s `currentYearSummary.CumulativeInflation = month.CumulativeInflation` where `month` is `projection[len(projection)-1]` at that year's close — the identical month index for both a full final year and a depletion-truncated one. No split-divisor path exists; confirmed by code inspection, not just trust.

5. Rendered-string arithmetic / x.5 ties. guardrails.html's template expression `mul 100.0 (div (sub a b) c)` and handlers.go's `(a-b)/c*100` are IEEE-754 commutative reorderings of the identical multiply — proved bit-identical with a throwaway `go run` probe at two crafted ties (7.5 and 11.5 from Multiplier/PreviousMultiplier pairs): `pct1==pct2` true both times, `%.0f` output identical ("7"/"7", "12"/"12"). template's `printf` is Go's builtin (== fmt.Sprintf), so this is provably a SINGLE formatter, not two that happen to agree by luck. The manifest's own `TestBuildGuardrailChartSummary` additionally asserts the full rendered string against fractional-cent money via `templates.FormatMoney`, matching ruling 2026-08-29b.

6. Dual formatters / JS money math. `grep toLocaleString\|Math.round` across charts.js and projection-chart.html: zero hits. All new-trace text (`Cut if balance ≤ ...`, `Raise if balance ≥ ...`, marker hover) is pre-composed server-side Go strings via `templates.FormatMoney`; yaxis2's `tickformat: "$,.0f"` is a declarative Plotly/D3 format string, not custom JS arithmetic. No dual-formatter surface exists for these figures.

7. Theme/height. `layoutAxisKeys` (charts.js:41-49) matches `/^[xy]axis\d*$/` against `Object.keys(serverLayout)`, which includes `yaxis2` whenever guardrails are enabled — dark-mode `gridcolor`/`tickfont.color` is applied to it via the same `themedLayoutUpdate` path used for yaxis. `themechange` handler (charts.js:614-635) never touches `height`, so the server-set `"height": 520` (handlers.go, new) survives every relayout; CSS adds `#chart-projection.chart-container-tall { min-height: 540px }` (styles.css) and the class is present on the container in projection-chart.html:35, giving the panel room without a TH-style collapse. OBSERVATION (not a criterion violation): `"height": 520` and the `chart-container-tall` class are applied UNCONDITIONALLY, i.e. even when guardrails are disabled — a single-panel balance chart is now always ~520/540px instead of whatever it rendered at before this task. The acceptance claim only constrains trace/yaxis2 presence when disabled, not height, so this is in-scope-compliant; flagging for the backlog as a minor UX footprint change for non-guardrails plans, not a defect.

8. Existing ProjectionYearSummary consumers. `grep` for the new field names and for the type across present_value.go, explainability.go, spending_trajectory.go, mcpsvc/plan/view.go: zero direct references (no blind-range risk). New fields are `omitempty` and zero-valued whenever `Guardrails` is nil/disabled (asserted by the manifest's `TestGuardrailTriggerLevelsZeroWhenDisabled`), so any JSON-shape assertion in those consumers is unaffected. My own independent full-suite run (`go test ./internal/models/ ./internal/services/... ./internal/handlers/...`) passed every package including `mcpsvc/plan` (11s) and `retirement/analysis` (55s) — this is the strongest available evidence these consumers are unaffected.

9. "Compare without guardrails" overlay. `handleWhatIfProjectionChartNoGuardrails` clones settings with `Guardrails = nil` before calling `buildProjectionChartData`, so `guardrailsEnabled` is false and only the balance trace (+ events trace, empty) comes back — confirmed by reading the handler. `toggleGuardrailCompareOverlay` in charts.js builds its overlay trace with no `yaxis` key, defaulting to the primary balance axis (y1) — the correct axis for a balance series, verified by reading the code (not driving a browser — noted as a reading-only check per the task's own allowance).

10. Mutation kill. Reverted the marker-relocation hunk in my copy (markers back to `projectionValueAtYear`/old-style placement instead of `afterGuardrailsY[monthIdx]`), rebuilt, ran `TestBuildProjectionChartDataGuardrailTriggerTraces` — it FAILED immediately (`marker 0 y=347921.55 != After-guardrails value 11070.00 at month 12`), confirming the manifest's permanent test is a real mutation-kill, not a name-only assertion. Restored the file afterward; rebuild confirmed clean.

Scope check: `git diff 5fae968 --stat` (excluding the `data` symlink) touches exactly handlers.go, models/whatif.go, engine/{month,stepper}.go, styles.css, projection-chart.html, guardrail_graph_test.go (GV2 manifest) plus handlers_guardrail_optimizer.go and guardrail_optimizer.html — both explicitly declared foreign territory (GV3 in flight: plan-floor notice, read and confirmed unrelated to chart/trigger logic) — and no files outside the manifest or declared foreign territories.

All ten attack surfaces refuted on this evidence; no in-scope defect found. PASS.
